# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# "What affects the cost" section for each service page, rendered after the main body, and the
# "Questions to ask before you hire" checklist used on the service + city pages.
# No prices are stated here: real pricing depends on the client's rates and each site visit.
# (The instant roof estimate uses the client's own price per square from site.config.json.)

COSTS = {
"roof-replacement": """<p>Roof replacement is priced by the square (100 square feet of roof surface), so size comes first. Pitch is next: steep roofs need more safety equipment and move more slowly, and the actual roof surface is larger than the house's footprint. The number of old layers to remove, valleys, dormers, skylights and chimneys all add labor and flashing. Shingle line matters too, from standard architectural shingles to designer styles. Decking that's rotted or too thin must be replaced before the new roof goes on, so we list a per-sheet price in advance instead of guessing.</p>""",
"metal-roofing": """<p>Metal roofing usually costs more up front than asphalt shingles, and the system you choose sets most of the price. Standing seam with concealed fasteners costs more than exposed-fastener panels. Panel gauge, finish and color all play a part. Complex roofs with hips, valleys and dormers need more cutting, custom trim and flashing, while simple gable roofs are the most economical. A tear-off, any deck repairs, high-temperature underlayment and snow guards are part of the total, which we itemize separately.</p>""",
"flat-roofing": """<p>Flat roof pricing depends on the roof's size, the membrane you choose and what's underneath. If old layers or wet insulation have to come off, or the deck is damaged, that work comes first. Adding tapered insulation to fix ponding costs more but often extends the life of the new roof. The number of penetrations, curbs, drains, walls and edges matters, because each one needs careful flashing. Access also counts: small rooftops reached through tight spaces take longer to work on than open roofs with room for a lift.</p>""",
"new-construction-roofing": """<p>On new construction, cost depends mostly on roof area, pitch and complexity. A simple gable roofs quickly, while hips, valleys, dormers and multiple roof heights add labor and flashing. Material choice, from architectural shingles to metal, is the next biggest factor. Ventilation products, eave protection depth, skylights and any special requirements in the drawings are priced to the plans. Schedule matters as well: a site that's ready when we arrive, with clear access for deliveries, keeps costs predictable.</p>""",
"commercial-roofing": """<p>Commercial roofing costs depend on the roof area, the membrane system, insulation requirements and the condition of the existing roof. Removing multiple old layers or wet insulation adds significant work. Rooftop equipment, curbs, drains and parapet walls each need detailed flashing. Access, safety requirements and working around tenants or business hours also affect labor. For repairs and maintenance, the extent of the problem and how easy it is to reach drive the cost. We provide a written scope so owners can compare options and plan budgets.</p>""",
"roof-repair": """<p>Most roof repairs are priced by the job rather than the square. The cost depends on what's failed, how much of the roof is affected and how hard it is to reach. Replacing a few shingles or a pipe boot is a modest job; re-flashing a chimney or re-lining a valley takes more time and materials. Steep or high roofs need extra safety set-up. If the deck is soft under the damaged area, that sheathing is replaced as part of the repair. We give you a written price before any work starts.</p>""",
"storm-damage-repair": """<p>Storm repair costs depend on how widespread the damage is and what was hit. A few missing shingles is a small repair; a tree through the roof can involve decking, rafters and interior work by other trades. Temporary protection, such as tarping, is priced separately from the permanent repair. If damage is scattered across the whole roof, replacement may be more practical than many individual repairs. Whether your insurance contributes depends on your policy and your insurer's decision, so we give you a clear written price either way.</p>""",
"roof-inspections": """<p>Inspection pricing depends on the size and type of roof, how steep and accessible it is and whether you need a simple condition check or a detailed written report with photos. Attic access, multiple roof sections and flat roofs that need closer examination take more time. If you're buying or selling a home, ask about a report you can share with the other party. If repairs are recommended, those are quoted separately in writing, and you're under no obligation to book them with us.</p>""",
"roof-ventilation": """<p>Ventilation costs depend on what your attic has now and what it needs. Adding a ridge vent during a roof replacement costs far less than retrofitting one later. Opening up blocked soffits, installing baffles or adding vented soffit panels adds labor at the roof edge. Venting bathroom fans properly to the outside may involve new ducting and a roof or wall cap. Removing competing vents and patching the roof is a small cost that pays off in better airflow. Attic insulation or air sealing, if needed, is quoted as a separate line.</p>""",
"gutters": """<p>Gutter pricing is based mainly on the total length of gutter and the number of downspouts. Larger 6-inch gutters cost more than standard 5-inch sizes but carry more water from big or steep roofs. Multiple stories, many corners and difficult access add labor. Gutter guards are a separate line, priced by length and guard type. If the fascia behind the gutters is rotted, it needs replacing first. Removing and disposing of the old system is included in the quote so there are no surprise charges.</p>""",
"soffit-fascia": """<p>Soffit and fascia work is priced by the length of the roofline and how much repair is needed underneath. Simple runs on a bungalow go quickly; multi-story homes, many corners and returns take more time and staging. Replacing rotted fascia boards or rafter tails before capping adds cost but prevents bigger problems later. Vented soffit, color choice and whether gutters are being removed and reinstalled or replaced all affect the total. We list repairs separately so you can see exactly what's being fixed.</p>""",
"skylights": """<p>Skylight costs depend on the unit, the opening and the roof. Replacing a skylight in an existing opening during a reroof is the most economical option. Venting or powered units cost more than fixed skylights, and larger sizes or special glass add to the price. Cutting a new opening involves framing, possibly a permit and finishing the interior light shaft. Sun tunnels are generally less expensive than full skylights. Each installation includes a manufacturer flashing kit matched to your roofing material.</p>""",
}

